      <h2>How it started</h2>
      <p>Morgan was diagnosed with celiac disease first, after years of being told her symptoms were "just stress" or "just IBS." A blood test and an endoscopy later, she finally had an answer — and a whole new way of eating to figure out, seemingly overnight.</p>
      <p>Less than two years later, Allie was diagnosed too. Celiac disease runs in families — first-degree relatives of someone with celiac have a significantly higher chance of developing it themselves, which is why doctors often recommend relatives get screened. For the sisters, it meant they weren't navigating this alone.</p>
      <p>What started as swapping restaurant recommendations and recipe fails in a group text turned into this website: the resource we wish we'd had on day one.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section-alt">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>What we're about</h2>
    <div class="grid grid-3">
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-body">
          <h3>Safety first</h3>
          <p>We only recommend restaurants and products based on what we've personally researched or eaten — including calling ahead, asking about shared fryers, and reading every label. If something isn't a dedicated gluten-free kitchen, we say so.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-body">
          <h3>No gatekeeping</h3>
          <p>Celiac disease is overwhelming enough without hunting for reliable information. Everything here is free, and we link out to the medical resources we trust.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-body">
          <h3>Still figuring it out</h3>
          <p>We don't have it all figured out either. We're two sisters in Chicago sharing what's worked for us — not doctors or dietitians. Always double check with your own care team.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

